      ],
      "publication_year": 2008,
      "publication_date": "2008-11-19",
      "abstract": "This thesis analyses literature's possibilities to give account of a highly reified event, the Holocaust. It examines three autobiographical novels of the Spanish-French writer Jorge Sempr\u00fan: \"Le grand voyage\" (1963), \"Quel beau dimanche!\" (1980), and \"L'\u00e9criture ou la vie\" (1994). There are two ideas that ground the conditions for such an analysis. First, the thesis subscribes a Hegelian Marxist description of Capitalism and the Holocaust. Second, it displays a Lukacsian concept of art, arguing that literature is a realm of freedom and authentic praxis. From the analyses of the texts the thesis concludes that Sempr\u00fan's novels give account of the specific subject-object relationship in Capitalism. The key concept that categorizes this relationship is \"reification\". However, only the last novel, \"L'\u00e9criture ou la vie\", manages to display an active stance that may simulate an authentic emergence of experience.",
